 CTO Africa", "payment platform migration"], "faq": [["What does the CBN localisation directive require?", "As reported, the Central Bank of Nigeria directed banks, fintechs and other payment service providers to store payment transaction data generated in Nigeria on servers in Nigeria from 1 January 2027, and said vendors with access to that data must align. Check the circular's exact wording with your legal team."], ["Does Nigeria's cloud policy require all data to be localised?", "Commentary on the National Digital Cloud Policy, adopted in 2026, describes a classification scheme with residency requirements for defined sovereign and sensitive categories rather than blanket localisation. Commercial personal data remains governed by the Nigeria Data Protection Act and sector rules, such as the CBN directive."], ["Do we need a separate platform for Nigeria?", "Usually not. A cell-based design runs the same code and deployment process in each location, with data pinned to the cell where rules require it. What changes is where each cell runs and which data may leave it."]], "related": ["/fractional-cto/", "/regions/", "/technical-due-diligence/"], "citations": [{"title": "Central Bank of Nigeria directs banks, fintechs to store payment data locally", "url": "https://www.zawya.com/en/economy/africa/central-bank-of-nigeria-directs-banks-fintechs-to-store-payment-data-locally-iwe1v186", "publisher": "Zawya", "date": "2026"}, {"title": "CBN's Data Localisation Mandate: What Nigerian Fintechs Need To Know", "url": "https://www.mondaq.com/nigeria/financial-services/1813412/cbns-data-localisation-mandate-what-nigerian-fintechs-need-to-know", "publisher": "Mondaq", "date": "2026"}, {"title": "Federal Government Unveils National Digital Cloud Policy to Drive Investment, Digital Sovereignty and Government Transformation", "url": "https://fmcide.gov.ng/federal-government-unveils-national-digital-cloud-policy-to-drive-investment-digital-sovereignty-and-government-transformation/", "publisher": "Federal Ministry of Communications, Innovation and Digital Economy (Nigeria)", "date": "2026"}, {"title": "Nigeria's National Digital Cloud Policy: Key Provisions And Implications For Business", "url": "https://www.legal500.com/intelligence/nigeria/technology/nigerias-national-digital-cloud-policy-key-provisions-and-implications-for-business", "publisher": "Legal 500", "date": "2026"}, {"title": "DIY: Create Your Own Cloud with Kubernetes (Part 3)", "url": "https://kubernetes.io/blog/2024/04/05/diy-create-your-own-cloud-with-kubernetes-part-3/", "publisher": "Kubernetes blog", "date": "5 Apr 2024"}]}-->
<h2>The situation</h2>
<p>A payments company headquartered in Lagos offered merchant collections, payouts and card processing in Nigeria, Kenya and Ghana. Nigeria was its largest market. Everything ran in one overseas public cloud region: one set of databases, one event stream, one analytics warehouse. It was simple to run, and every market shared the same release process.</p>
<p><strong>Why now.</strong> The Central Bank of Nigeria directed banks, fintechs and other payment service providers to store payment transaction data generated in Nigeria on servers in Nigeria from 1 January 2027, with vendors that handle that data expected to align<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. Legal commentary noted open questions, such as how "generated in Nigeria" applies to transactions that cross borders<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>. Separately, the federal government adopted a National Digital Cloud Policy in 2026, which commentators describe as classifying data and applying residency rules to defined sensitive and sovereign categories rather than all data<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>. With only months to the deadline, the company needed a plan it could execute, not a debate about interpretation.</p>

<h2>What had to be true</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Nigerian payment transaction data at rest and in processing in Nigeria</strong> by the deadline, including backups and logs.</li>
<li><strong>One platform</strong>: the same code, pipelines and on-call for every market.</li>
<li><strong>Every field classified</strong>, so engineers know what may leave a country and in what form.</li>
<li><strong>No lost or duplicated payments</strong> during migration.</li>
<li><strong>Vendors aligned</strong>: fraud, card and support tools that touch Nigerian payment data had to follow it.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Architecture</h2>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-stack">
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Global control</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">Management cluster<em>Cluster API and GitOps</em></span><span>Shared services<em>no payment data</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Nigeria cell</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">Onshore data centre<em>payments, ledger, logs</em></span><span>Tokenisation<em>tokens only leave</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Kenya and Ghana cells</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Cloud region<em>same code, own data</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Cross-border layer</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">Allowed exports<em>tokens, aggregates, metrics</em></span><span>Policy checks<em>in the pipeline</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Group functions</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Analytics<em>aggregates only</em></span><span>Fraud models<em>trained per cell</em></span><span>Observability<em>no payload data</em></span></div></div>
</div>
<figcaption>One control plane, data pinned to cells. Only classified, permitted data crosses borders.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2>The data flow, end to end</h2>
<figure class="dgm">
<ol class="dgm-flow g3">
<li><b>Route</b><span>The edge routes each request to the cell for its market</span></li>
<li><b>Process</b><span>Authorisation, ledger and settlement run inside the Nigeria cell</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>Store</b><span>Transactions, logs and backups stay on Nigerian infrastructure</span></li>
<li><b>Tokenise</b><span>Identifiers that must be shared are replaced with tokens</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>Export</b><span>Only classified, permitted aggregates and metrics leave the cell</span></li>
<li><b>Report</b><span>Group analytics and dashboards work from those exports</span></li>
</ol>
<figcaption>Processing and storage stay local; reporting works from what is allowed to travel.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2>The hard part: one platform, several homes</h2>
<p>The tempting answer was to fork: copy the platform into a Nigerian data centre and run it separately. That doubles releases, on-call and drift. Instead, the team adopted a cell design. Each market runs as a cell with identical services, deployed from the same Git repository. The cells differ only in where they run and in the data rules applied to them.</p>
<p>Cluster API made that practical across very different infrastructure. A management cluster declares the workload clusters, whether they run in a Nigerian colocation facility or a public cloud region, and the same tooling creates, upgrades and replaces them<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>. The management cluster holds no payment data, so it can sit outside Nigeria.</p>
<figure class="fig"><div class="im"><img src="/assets/img/nigeria-payment-data-localisation-platform/cluster-api.webp" alt="Diagram of a management Kubernetes cluster running Cluster API, which creates and manages three tenant Kubernetes clusters" width="1100" height="524" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption>One management cluster, many workload clusters: the pattern that kept every market on the same platform. <span class="credit">Source: <a href="https://kubernetes.io/blog/2024/04/05/diy-create-your-own-cloud-with-kubernetes-part-3/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kubernetes blog, Andrei Kvapil</a> · CC BY 4.0</span></figcaption></figure>
<p>The second hard part was the data no one thinks about: logs, traces, backups, support tickets and fraud vendor feeds. The team classified fields into a short list of classes, from public to payment transaction data, and enforced the classes in code: telemetry carried identifiers as tokens, backups were written to onshore storage only, and export pipelines refused unclassified fields. See <a href="/insights/data-contracts-playbook/">data contracts</a> for how to make that stick.</p>
<div class="callout"><p>Localisation fails in the side channels. Classify logs, traces, backups and vendor feeds as carefully as the main database.</p></div>

<h2>How it ran</h2>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-lanes">
<div class="dgm-lane"><small>Compliance and legal</small><ol><li>Directive interpretation</li><li class="hl">Data classes</li><li>Vendor terms</li></ol></div>
<div class="dgm-lane"><small>Platform engineering</small><ol><li>Onshore facility</li><li class="hl">Cluster API and GitOps</li><li>Backups and DR</li></ol></div>
<div class="dgm-lane"><small>Payments engineering</small><ol><li>Replication</li><li class="hl">Product-by-product cutover</li><li>Reconciliation</li></ol></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Legal defined the classes, platform built the home, payments moved the money flows.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-phases">
<div><small>Phase 1</small><b>Classify</b><ul><li>Field inventory</li><li>Data classes</li><li>Vendor review</li></ul></div>
<div><small>Phase 2</small><b>Build</b><ul><li>Onshore cell</li><li>Management cluster</li><li>Tokenisation service</li></ul></div>
<div><small>Phase 3</small><b>Replicate</b><ul><li>Logical replication</li><li>Dual-run checks</li><li>Reconciliation reports</li></ul></div>
<div><small>Phase 4</small><b>Cut over</b><ul><li>Per product</li><li>Rollback rehearsed</li><li>Old copies removed</li></ul></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Classify first: it decides what has to move.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>Migration ran product by product, starting with payouts, which had the simplest flows. Databases were replicated into the Nigeria cell and both copies ran side by side, with reconciliation reports comparing every transaction, before traffic moved. Payment idempotency mattered here; see <a href="/insights/idempotent-payments-order-pipelines/">idempotent payment pipelines</a>. After each cutover, overseas copies and backups were deleted on a recorded schedule.</p>

<h2>Key decisions and trade-offs</h2>
<table class="tbl">
<thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Alternative</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Cells on one codebase</td><td>Fork a Nigerian platform</td><td>One release process and one on-call</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cluster API across colocation and cloud</td><td>Different tooling per location</td><td>Same lifecycle everywhere</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tokens and aggregates cross borders</td><td>Copy full records to the group warehouse</td><td>Group reporting without moving payment data</td></tr>
<tr><td>Product-by-product cutover</td><td>One big switch</td><td>Smaller blast radius and real rollback</td></tr>
<tr><td>Classify telemetry and backups</td><td>Focus on primary databases</td><td>Side channels are where localisation breaks</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h2>What I'd do again, and differently</h2>
<p>Again: classify before building. The classes answered most of the hard questions, including which vendors were affected. Differently: involve card and fraud vendors earlier. Their onshore options and timelines set the critical path more than our own engineering did. For related patterns, see <a href="/case-studies/multi-cloud-terraform-estate/">a multi-cloud Terraform estate</a>, <a href="/insights/kubernetes-multi-tenancy/">Kubernetes multi-tenancy</a> and <a href="/regions/">where I work</a>.</p>
<p>If you run payments or financial data across African markets, see my <a href="/fractional-cto/">fractional CTO work</a>, or <a href="/#assess">analyse your challenge</a> and bring the result to a call.</p>
